    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Schistosomiasis is a parasitic disease that affects over 200 million humans across the globe, largely in sub-Saharan Africa. It is transmitted between humans and various species of freshwater host snails. One possible technique for reducing disease transmission is biological control: introducing competitors or predators of host snails to suppress their population and reduce transmission. However, little is known about the effect of parasitic infection on predator-prey interaction, especially in the case of schistosomiasis. This study details laboratory tests performed on B. truncatus and B. glabrata snails to investigate both behavioral differences between uninfected snails and those infected with schistosomiasis, and differences in consumption based on infection status by the snail predator M. vollenhovenii. Predation trials with these prawns showed a statistically significant preference for consuming snails infected with schistosomiasis. Behavioral trials aimed at explaining this preference showed infected snails to be more sluggish and less likely to avoid predation by exiting the water and hiding under vinyl substrate, providing a rationale for why infected snails were more readily available for consumption. These results both suggest increased efficacy of predation as a means of control for schistosomiasis infection in humans and provide greater insight into the effects of parasitic infection on predator-prey dynamics.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
